WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.950
And the last group of food: lipids.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.950
Lipids are digested by enzymes called lipases into glycerol and fatty acids, but it’s not an easy job.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.950
It requires a process called emulsification to take place first.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.950
Emulsification breaks the lipids into smaller droplets.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.950
The smaller droplets have a larger surface area for the lipase enzymes to work on.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.950
To learn more about bile and emulsification watch this video.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.950
So to recap the main enzymes involved in digestion:

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.950
[Music]

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.950
A carbohydrase called amylase is released in the saliva.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.950
This digests starch into smaller sugars.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.950
A protease called pepsin digests protein in the stomach,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.950
then a protease called trypsin further digests proteins into amino acids in the small intestine.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.950
A carbohydrase called amylase, and lipase, are also released into the small intestine from the pancreas.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.950
They digest carbohydrates into sugars and lipids into fatty acids and glycerol.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.950
These molecules are small enough to be absorbed into the blood.

